  questions: [
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-001",
      text: "A teaching collection contains the 22 bones of the adult skull proper, all six auditory ossicles and the single hyoid. How many bones are present?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "22 bones" }, { key: "b", text: "28 bones" }, { key: "c", text: "29 bones" }, { key: "d", text: "30 bones" }],
      answer: "c",
      explanation: "The stated collection contains 22 + 6 + 1 = 29 bones. Twenty-eight would count the skull and auditory ossicles but omit the hyoid. The skull-proper convention of 22 should not be confused with a regional count that deliberately includes additional nearby bones.",
      sectionId: "sk-regional-counts", source: { document: "biology", page: 91 }, cognitive: "application"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-002",
      text: "A student counts every carpal in both normal adult human wrists, including each pisiform. What total should be recorded?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "16 carpals" }, { key: "b", text: "14 carpals" }, { key: "c", text: "18 carpals" }, { key: "d", text: "10 carpals" }],
      answer: "a",
      explanation: "Each human wrist contains eight carpals, including pisiform, so both wrists contain 8 multiplied by 2 = 16. Adding pisiform again would incorrectly produce eighteen. Fourteen confuses the carpal count with the phalangeal count of one hand.",
      sectionId: "sk-hand-bones", source: { document: "quick-revision", page: 178 }, cognitive: "application"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-003",
      text: "A regional thoracic-cage inventory includes 24 ribs, one sternum and twelve thoracic vertebrae. What is its total without adding any cartilage as bone?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "25 bones" }, { key: "b", text: "36 bones" }, { key: "c", text: "38 bones" }, { key: "d", text: "37 bones" }],
      answer: "d",
      explanation: "The inventory is 24 + 1 + 12 = 37 bones. Twenty-five would omit the thoracic vertebrae. When later counting the whole axial skeleton, these same twelve vertebrae already belong to the 26-bone vertebral-column total and must not be counted twice.",
      sectionId: "sk-regional-counts", source: { document: "biology", page: 92 }, cognitive: "application"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-004",
      text: "An adult inventory contains the two free lower limbs, 30 bones each, and the two fused hip bones. Which total corrects a chart that reports 66?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "60 bones" }, { key: "b", text: "62 bones" }, { key: "c", text: "64 bones" }, { key: "d", text: "68 bones" }],
      answer: "b",
      explanation: "Two free lower limbs give 2 multiplied by 30 = 60. Two adult hip bones add two, giving 62. Counting ilium, ischium and pubis separately on each side produces six developmental components and mixes that convention with an adult bone count.",
      sectionId: "sk-regional-counts", source: { document: "biology", page: 94 }, cognitive: "application"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-005",
      text: "Which human bone supports tongue and floor-of-mouth attachments while remaining suspended without a direct bony articulation?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Hyoid" }, { key: "b", text: "Mandible" }, { key: "c", text: "Clavicle" }, { key: "d", text: "Vomer" }],
      answer: "a",
      explanation: "The hyoid is suspended by muscles and ligaments rather than forming a direct joint with another bone. It supports tongue and floor-of-mouth attachments. The mandible articulates with temporal bone, the clavicle has sternal and scapular articulations, and the vomer forms part of the nasal septum.",
      sectionId: "sk-hyoid-and-ossicles", source: { document: "biology", page: 91 }, cognitive: "recall"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-006",
      text: "A skull-base label points to a single butterfly-shaped bone that articulates with all the other cranial bones. Which name belongs on the label?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Temporal bone" }, { key: "b", text: "Frontal bone" }, { key: "c", text: "Ethmoid bone" }, { key: "d", text: "Sphenoid bone" }],
      answer: "d",
      explanation: "The sphenoid is the single butterfly-shaped cranial-base bone and contacts all the other cranial bones. Temporal bones are paired, while frontal and ethmoid do not match the complete description. The articulation claim concerns the cranium, not every facial bone of the whole skull.",
      sectionId: "sk-skull-bones", source: { document: "biology", page: 91 }, cognitive: "recall"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-007",
      text: "A mandibular condyle moves in its joint during chewing. Which bone supplies the fossa on the opposite side of this articulation?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "The maxilla" }, { key: "b", text: "The temporal bone" }, { key: "c", text: "The sphenoid" }, { key: "d", text: "The palatine bone" }],
      answer: "b",
      explanation: "The mandibular condyle articulates with the temporal bone at the TMJ, which includes an articular disc. Upper and lower teeth contact during chewing, but that does not make the maxilla the bone forming the mandibular fossa or a direct synovial partner of the mandible.",
      sectionId: "sk-mandible-and-cranial-base", source: { document: "biology", page: 92 }, cognitive: "application"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-008",
      text: "In the mnemonic MIS HAS, the middle ossicle must be paired with its shape. Which pairing occupies that middle position?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Malleus with stirrup" }, { key: "b", text: "Stapes with hammer" }, { key: "c", text: "Incus with anvil" }, { key: "d", text: "Incus with stirrup" }],
      answer: "c",
      explanation: "MIS denotes malleus, incus and stapes, while HAS denotes hammer, anvil and stirrup in the same order. Therefore the middle pair is incus-anvil. Each human middle ear has one of each, so this is an identity mnemonic rather than a numerical formula.",
      sectionId: "sk-hyoid-and-ossicles", source: { document: "biology", page: 92 }, cognitive: "recall"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-009",
      text: "Two rounded surfaces beside the foramen magnum receive the superior facets of the atlas. To which bone do these surfaces belong?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Sphenoid bone" }, { key: "b", text: "Temporal bone" }, { key: "c", text: "Parietal bone" }, { key: "d", text: "Occipital bone" }],
      answer: "d",
      explanation: "The paired occipital condyles flank the foramen magnum and articulate with atlas. They are bony articular projections, not parts of the brain's occipital lobe. This skull-atlas articulation is distinct from the dens-atlas pivot immediately below it.",
      sectionId: "sk-mandible-and-cranial-base", source: { document: "biology", page: 92 }, cognitive: "recall"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-010",
      text: "Which pairing distinguishes the main upper-neck movements of nodding yes and turning the head to indicate no?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Yes at median atlanto-axial; no at atlanto-occipital" }, { key: "b", text: "Yes and no chiefly at the atlanto-occipital joints" }, { key: "c", text: "Yes at atlanto-occipital; no at median atlanto-axial" }, { key: "d", text: "Yes and no chiefly at the median atlanto-axial joint" }],
      answer: "c",
      explanation: "The occipital condyles and atlas permit chiefly flexion-extension associated with nodding. Rotation chiefly occurs around the axis dens at the median atlanto-axial pivot. Distinguishing these adjacent contacts is more accurate than assigning all head movement to one joint type.",
      sectionId: "sk-cervical-atlas-axis", source: { document: "biology", page: 93 }, cognitive: "understanding"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-011",
      text: "A vertebral artery follows the usual human route and first enters the series of cervical transverse foramina at which level?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "C6 level" }, { key: "b", text: "C5 level" }, { key: "c", text: "C4 level" }, { key: "d", text: "C7 level" }],
      answer: "a",
      explanation: "The vertebral artery usually enters the transverse-foramen series at C6. Variant entry levels occur, which is why the stem specifies the usual route. C7 commonly still has transverse foramina: absence of the artery from an opening does not establish absence of the opening itself.",
      sectionId: "sk-cervical-atlas-axis", source: { document: "quick-revision", page: 177 }, cognitive: "recall"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-012",
      text: "An exiting spinal nerve is compressed in the opening between adjacent vertebrae, lateral to the central canal. Which opening is involved?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "The foramen magnum" }, { key: "b", text: "An intervertebral foramen" }, { key: "c", text: "A transverse foramen" }, { key: "d", text: "The obturator foramen" }],
      answer: "b",
      explanation: "Spinal nerves exit through intervertebral foramina formed between adjacent vertebrae. The vertebral canal is the central longitudinal passage, while cervical transverse foramina have vascular relationships. The foramen magnum and obturator foramen occur at entirely different regional locations.",
      sectionId: "sk-vertebral-structure", source: { document: "biology", page: 93 }, cognitive: "application"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-013",
      text: "A comparative specimen has a vertebral centrum concave at both its anterior and posterior ends. Which term describes this shape?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Procoelous" }, { key: "b", text: "Amphicoelous" }, { key: "c", text: "Amphicondylous" }, { key: "d", text: "Opisthocoelous" }],
      answer: "b",
      explanation: "Amphicoelous means biconcave. Procoelous and opisthocoelous each have one concave and one convex end, in opposite orientations. Amphicondylous means biconvex, making it the opposite of the specimen described rather than another name for it.",
      sectionId: "sk-centrum-shapes", source: { document: "biology", page: 93 }, cognitive: "understanding"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-014",
      text: "A diagram marks a centrum's anterior face as concave and its posterior face as convex. Which label preserves that orientation?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Procoelous" }, { key: "b", text: "Opisthocoelous" }, { key: "c", text: "Acoelous" }, { key: "d", text: "Amphicoelous" }],
      answer: "a",
      explanation: "Procoelous specifies anterior concavity and posterior convexity. Reversing the ends gives opisthocoelous. Acoelous means approximately flat at both ends and amphicoelous concave at both, so neither preserves the two different surfaces in the stated orientation.",
      sectionId: "sk-centrum-shapes", source: { document: "biology", page: 93 }, cognitive: "understanding"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-015",
      text: "Using the conventional 33-segment human vertebral formula, fusion leaves one sacrum and one coccyx. By how many does the standard adult bone count fall?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "4 bones" }, { key: "b", text: "5 bones" }, { key: "c", text: "7 bones" }, { key: "d", text: "9 bones" }],
      answer: "c",
      explanation: "Five sacral segments becoming one reduce the count by four, and four coccygeal segments becoming one reduce it by three. The combined reduction is 4 + 3 = 7, leaving 33 - 7 = 26 bones under the stated conventional adult count.",
      sectionId: "sk-vertebral-counts", source: { document: "biology", page: 92 }, cognitive: "application"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-016",
      text: "A giraffe and a human normally share the same cervical-vertebra count. What chiefly explains their markedly different neck lengths?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Fusion of human thoracic vertebrae into the neck" }, { key: "b", text: "Replacement of giraffe cervical bone by cartilage" }, { key: "c", text: "Counting giraffe ribs as extra cervical vertebrae" }, { key: "d", text: "Different lengths of the cervical vertebrae" }],
      answer: "d",
      explanation: "Both normally have seven cervical vertebrae, but the giraffe's are greatly elongated. This demonstrates that number and dimensions are different anatomical variables. Seven is the usual mammalian pattern, not an exceptionless rule for every mammal species.",
      sectionId: "sk-cervical-atlas-axis", source: { document: "quick-revision", page: 177 }, cognitive: "understanding"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-017",
      text: "A label identifies the shallow socket receiving the humeral head, not the fossa receiving the mandibular condyle. Which location fits?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Glenoid fossa of the temporal bone" }, { key: "b", text: "Acetabulum of the hip bone" }, { key: "c", text: "Glenoid cavity of the scapula" }, { key: "d", text: "Coronoid fossa of the humerus" }],
      answer: "c",
      explanation: "The scapular glenoid cavity receives the humeral head at the shoulder. The similarly named temporal glenoid or mandibular fossa receives the mandibular condyle. The acetabulum receives the femoral head, and the coronoid fossa is a humeral depression at the elbow.",
      sectionId: "sk-pectoral-girdle", source: { document: "biology", page: 94 }, cognitive: "application"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-018",
      text: "A humeral ridge provides insertion for the deltoid muscle. Which similarly named structure must be kept separate from this upper-arm landmark?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "The bicipital groove of the humerus" }, { key: "b", text: "The deltoid ligament at the ankle" }, { key: "c", text: "The olecranon fossa of the humerus" }, { key: "d", text: "The coracoid process of the scapula" }],
      answer: "b",
      explanation: "Deltoid ridge or tuberosity is a humeral muscle-attachment landmark, whereas the deltoid ligament supports the medial ankle. Their shared word does not imply a shared location or tissue type. The other options are different named landmarks rather than the misleading deltoid namesake.",
      sectionId: "sk-humerus", source: { document: "quick-revision", page: 178 }, cognitive: "understanding"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-019",
      text: "During elbow extension, a proximal forearm projection enters a posterior depression of the humerus. Which projection is it?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Styloid process of the radius" }, { key: "b", text: "Coracoid process of the scapula" }, { key: "c", text: "Coronoid process of the mandible" }, { key: "d", text: "Olecranon process of the ulna" }],
      answer: "d",
      explanation: "The ulnar olecranon enters the humeral olecranon fossa as the elbow extends. A process is the projection, and a fossa is the receiving depression. The radial styloid is at the wrist, while scapular and mandibular processes belong to other regions.",
      sectionId: "sk-radius-and-ulna", source: { document: "quick-revision", page: 178 }, cognitive: "recall"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-020",
      text: "Turning the palm downward involves the radius rotating relative to the ulna. Which synovial joint type chiefly permits this forearm action?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Pivot at the radioulnar articulations" }, { key: "b", text: "Hinge at the humeroulnar articulation" }, { key: "c", text: "Ellipsoid at the radiocarpal articulation" }, { key: "d", text: "Plane at the intercarpal articulations" }],
      answer: "a",
      explanation: "Pronation and supination involve rotation at the proximal and distal radioulnar pivot joints. Elbow flexion-extension and wrist bending or gliding occur at different articulations. All the listed joint types have genuine anatomical examples, but only the radioulnar pivots supply the described relative forearm rotation.",
      sectionId: "sk-radius-and-ulna", source: { document: "biology", page: 94 }, cognitive: "application"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-021",
      text: "Which description correctly distinguishes the fibula from the tibia in a human leg?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Fibula is lateral and slender, with an important stabilising role" }, { key: "b", text: "Fibula is medial and carries most axial load from the femur" }, { key: "c", text: "Fibula is lateral and forms the main leg articulation with the femur" }, { key: "d", text: "Fibula is medial and has the larger upper articular plateau" }],
      answer: "a",
      explanation: "The fibula is the lateral slender leg bone and contributes to muscle attachment and ankle stability. The tibia is medial, has the large proximal plateau and is the major weight-transmitting leg bone articulating with the femur. Lateral position therefore does not make the fibula the main femoral load receiver.",
      sectionId: "sk-lower-limb-bones", source: { document: "biology", page: 94 }, cognitive: "recall"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-022",
      text: "A femur is labelled at its head, greater trochanter and patellar surface. Which label identifies the part received by the hip socket?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Greater trochanter" }, { key: "b", text: "Patellar surface" }, { key: "c", text: "Femoral head" }, { key: "d", text: "Intercondylar fossa" }],
      answer: "c",
      explanation: "The femoral head is the ball received by the acetabulum. Trochanters provide muscle-attachment areas, and the patellar surface guides the patella at the distal anterior femur. The posterior intercondylar fossa also belongs to the distal knee region, not the hip socket.",
      sectionId: "sk-lower-limb-bones", source: { document: "quick-revision", page: 177 }, cognitive: "recall"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-023",
      text: "One normal human foot has seven tarsals, five metatarsals and fourteen phalanges. How many bones does that foot contain, excluding tibia, fibula and patella?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "25 bones" }, { key: "b", text: "26 bones" }, { key: "c", text: "27 bones" }, { key: "d", text: "30 bones" }],
      answer: "b",
      explanation: "Adding the stated foot components gives 7 + 5 + 14 = 26. Thirty is the count for the entire free lower limb once femur, patella, tibia and fibula are included. This also exposes the source typo of six rather than five metatarsals.",
      sectionId: "sk-lower-limb-bones", source: { document: "biology", page: 94 }, cognitive: "application"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-024",
      text: "A five-position phalangeal chart reads 0-2-2-3-3 for a forelimb. What does the leading zero record within this chart's convention?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "The absence of every forelimb carpal" }, { key: "b", text: "The presence of only one forelimb bone" }, { key: "c", text: "The fusion of all phalanges into one bone" }, { key: "d", text: "The absence of a digit in the first position" }],
      answer: "d",
      explanation: "Each position records phalanges in a successive digit, so zero indicates an absent first-position digit under the stated convention. The remaining digits have 2 + 2 + 3 + 3 = 10 phalanges. The formula does not count carpals or all bones in the limb.",
      sectionId: "sk-digital-formulae", source: { document: "biology", page: 94 }, cognitive: "understanding"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-025",
      text: "Opposition of the thumb depends strongly on the specialised joint between the trapezium and first metacarpal. Which surface arrangement characterises that joint?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "A peg rotating inside a ligamentous ring" }, { key: "b", text: "Two flat surfaces allowing only small glides" }, { key: "c", text: "A deep spherical socket around a rounded head" }, { key: "d", text: "Reciprocally concave-convex saddle surfaces" }],
      answer: "d",
      explanation: "The first carpometacarpal joint is the classic saddle joint, allowing the biaxial movements that contribute to thumb opposition. It is not the thumb's interphalangeal hinge. Specifying the trapezium-first-metacarpal contact avoids treating all thumb joints as one type.",
      sectionId: "sk-synovial-types", source: { document: "quick-revision", page: 178 }, cognitive: "understanding"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-026",
      text: "A joint contains an articular disc of fibrocartilage but also has a capsule and synovial cavities. Why is the TMJ still classified as synovial?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Every joint containing any cartilage is freely movable" }, { key: "b", text: "Its cavity and capsule define the structural arrangement" }, { key: "c", text: "Fibrocartilage is another name for synovial membrane" }, { key: "d", text: "The mandibular condyle has no articular covering" }],
      answer: "b",
      explanation: "Synovial classification depends on the joint's organised cavity, capsule and lining, not merely the presence or absence of a cartilage type. The TMJ has fibrocartilaginous components but remains synovial. A pubic symphysis lacks that synovial-cavity arrangement despite also containing fibrocartilage.",
      sectionId: "sk-joint-classification", source: { document: "biology", page: 95 }, cognitive: "understanding"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-027",
      text: "A student labels every connection between tibia and fibula as fibrous. Which regional distinction corrects that claim?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Both proximal and distal contacts are fibrous syndesmoses" }, { key: "b", text: "The proximal joint is a syndesmosis; the distal is synovial" }, { key: "c", text: "The proximal joint is synovial; the distal is a syndesmosis" }, { key: "d", text: "The proximal joint is synovial; the distal is a symphysis" }],
      answer: "c",
      explanation: "The proximal tibiofibular joint is a synovial articulation, while the distal tibiofibular connection is a fibrous syndesmosis. The interosseous membrane is also fibrous. Naming the two bones alone therefore cannot uniquely establish one structural class for every contact between them.",
      sectionId: "sk-joint-classification", source: { document: "biology", page: 95 }, cognitive: "understanding"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-028",
      text: "Two neighbouring vertebrae have both a disc between their bodies and paired facet articulations. Which classification pairing is correct?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Body connection: symphysis; facet contacts: plane synovial" }, { key: "b", text: "Body connection: synchondrosis; facet contacts: plane synovial" }, { key: "c", text: "Body connection: symphysis; facet contacts: fibrous syndesmosis" }, { key: "d", text: "Body connection: plane synovial; facet contacts: symphysis" }],
      answer: "a",
      explanation: "The intervertebral disc participates in a secondary cartilaginous symphysis between bodies. The zygapophyseal facets form plane synovial joints. These are different anatomical contacts, so both correct classifications can occur between the same neighbouring vertebrae without contradiction.",
      sectionId: "sk-synovial-types", source: { document: "biology", page: 95 }, cognitive: "application"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-029",
      text: "A gout explanation states that deposited synovial fluid causes a painful inflammatory attack. What material should replace synovial fluid in that explanation?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Monosodium urate crystals" }, { key: "b", text: "Calcium pyrophosphate crystals" }, { key: "c", text: "Hydroxyapatite crystals" }, { key: "d", text: "Cholesterol crystals" }],
      answer: "a",
      explanation: "Gout involves an inflammatory response to monosodium urate crystals deposited in and around joints. Synovial fluid can contain those crystals, but ordinary joint fluid is not itself the offending deposit. Hyperuricaemia raises the risk without guaranteeing that every person develops gout.",
      sectionId: "sk-arthritis-and-gout", source: { document: "biology", page: 96 }, cognitive: "understanding"
    },
    {
      id: "note-skeleton-030",
      text: "An adult has reduced bone mass and disrupted trabecular architecture, but the remaining bone is generally normally mineralised. Which distinction best fits this description?",
      options: [{ key: "a", text: "Osteomalacia with defective osteoid mineralisation" }, { key: "b", text: "Osteoporosis with loss of normally mineralised bone" }, { key: "c", text: "Rickets with defective growth-plate mineralisation" }, { key: "d", text: "Rheumatoid arthritis with primary autoimmune synovitis" }],
      answer: "b",
      explanation: "Osteoporosis chiefly involves reduced bone quantity and impaired microarchitecture with generally normal mineralisation of remaining bone. Osteomalacia is defective mineralisation of osteoid. Distinguishing amount from mineralisation avoids interpreting every weak adult skeleton as vitamin D-deficiency osteomalacia.",
      sectionId: "sk-bone-disorders", source: { document: "biology", page: 96 }, cognitive: "application"
    }
  ]
